course: biology
lesson: 269
title: Feathered dinosaurs: from Archaeopteryx to the chicken on your plate
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Birds are living dinosaurs: they come from small, feathered, meat-eating dinosaurs, so the drumstick on your plate really is a dinosaur leg.
explain: Archaeopteryx, 150 million years old and found in Germany in 1861, had wings and feathers like a bird but teeth, claws and a bony tail like a dinosaur, just the in-between kind Darwin's idea expected.
explain: Since 1996 thousands of feathered dinosaurs have turned up in China, kept in fine volcanic ash, and one, Sinosauropteryx, even had a striped orange-brown tail.
explain: The first feathers were simple fluff like a chick's down, for warmth; later they grew bigger for showing off, and only last of all were they used to fly.
explain: Snap the wishbone of the Sunday chicken and you hold the same two joined collarbones that T. rex and Velociraptor had.
fact: Birds are living dinosaurs, descended from small feathered meat-eating dinosaurs, and there are about 11,000 kinds of birds alive today.
fact: Birds and many dinosaurs share hollow bones, wishbones, three-toed feet and feathers.
fact: Archaeopteryx, about 150 million years old, had feathered wings but also teeth, claws and a long bony tail; it was found in Germany in 1861, soon after Darwin's book.
fact: Thousands of feathered dinosaur fossils have been found in China since 1996, kept by fine volcanic ash; Sinosauropteryx had a striped orange-brown tail.
fact: In 2016 a feathered dinosaur tail was reported in amber from Myanmar, with feathers and bones kept in 3D; amber is hardened tree resin.
fact: The first feathers were simple threads like a chick's down; they kept animals warm, then grew bigger for show, and only later were used for flight.
fact: The wishbone is two collarbones joined together; T. rex and Velociraptor had one too, and birds use it like a spring when flapping.
fact: A chicken walks on two legs with its body level, much like small dinosaurs; when scientists stuck a fake tail on chickens, they walked even more like dinosaurs.
fact: The cassowary is a big flightless bird with a crest, dagger-like claws and a deep booming call, often called the living bird that looks most like a dinosaur; it can be dangerous, so people should never go close.
fact: Crocodiles and birds share a common ancestor, so a crocodile is a closer cousin to a sparrow than to a lizard or a gecko.
fact: The birds that survived the asteroid were probably small ground birds that ate seeds, because seeds lasted in the ground while forests died.
fact: Early birds such as Hesperornis and Ichthyornis had teeth; modern birds lost their teeth and grew light beaks, which helps them fly.
exercise: Which living animals are dinosaurs? | Birds | Lizards | Crocodiles | Frogs
exercise: About how many kinds of birds are alive today? | About 11,000 | About 11 | About 110 | About 11 million
exercise: What did Archaeopteryx have that modern birds do not? | Teeth, claws on its wings and a long bony tail | Feathers, wings and a pair of scaly legs with toes | Two legs, two eyes and a hard pointed beak | A wishbone made of two joined collarbones
exercise: Where and when was Archaeopteryx found? | In Germany in 1861 | In China in 1996 | In America in 1990 | In Egypt 2,000 years ago
exercise: Where have thousands of feathered dinosaur fossils been found since 1996? | In China | In Antarctica | Under the sea near Hawaii | On the Moon
exercise: What kept the feathers of the Chinese dinosaurs? | Fine volcanic ash | Thick blue ice | Salty sea water | Sticky honey
exercise: What colour pattern did Sinosauropteryx have on its tail? | Orange-brown stripes | Bright blue spots | Pure white | Green and gold squares
exercise: What is amber? | Hardened tree resin | A kind of bone | Frozen water | Melted glass from a volcano
exercise: What were the first feathers like? | Simple threads like a chick's down | Big flight feathers like an eagle's | Hard scales | Long hairs like a horse's tail
exercise: What were the first feathers mainly for? | Keeping warm | Flying high | Swimming fast | Digging holes
exercise: What is a wishbone? | Two collarbones joined together | A spare rib at the bottom of the chest | A small piece of the skull | A toe bone that points backwards
exercise: Which of these dinosaurs had a wishbone? | T. rex | No dinosaur at all | Only Stegosaurus | Only Triceratops
exercise: What happened when scientists put a fake tail on chickens? | They walked even more like dinosaurs | They started to fly higher and better | They fell over at once and could not stand | They laid much bigger eggs than before
exercise: Which living bird looks most like a dinosaur? | The cassowary | The robin | The grey parrot | The emperor penguin
exercise: Which is a crocodile's closer cousin? | A sparrow | A gecko | A grass snake | A turtle
exercise: Which birds probably survived the asteroid? | Small ground birds that ate seeds | Big tree-living birds | Birds that ate only fruit from tall trees | No birds survived
exercise: Why did modern birds swap teeth for beaks? | Beaks are light, which helps flight | Their teeth fell out from eating sweet fruit | Beaks are better than teeth for chewing meat | Teeth got too cold up in the sky
test: «eating chicken nuggets = eating dinosaur, lol not really tho» What is right? | Really: chickens are living dinosaurs | No, chickens are a kind of lizard with feathers | No, chickens are mammals, like cows | Only turkey nuggets count, not chicken
test: In a cartoon the dinosaurs are all scaly and none has a feather. What would a new science book show? | Many dinosaurs with feathers or fluff | Dinosaurs with fur like cats | Dinosaurs made of rock | Only scales, as in the cartoon
test: «a crocodile's basically a big lizard right» What is true? | No, crocodiles are closer to birds than to lizards | Yes, it is just a giant lizard that lives in rivers | Yes, crocs come from snakes that grew legs | No, crocodiles are fish that learnt to walk
test: Count them: Mum puts 2 roast chickens on the table. How many drumsticks and wishbones are there? | 4 drumsticks and 2 wishbones | 2 drumsticks and 4 wishbones | 4 drumsticks and 4 wishbones | 8 drumsticks and 2 wishbones
test: What would happen if you walked up to a wild cassowary to pat it? | It could attack with its dagger-like claws, so stay away | It would kneel down and let you climb on and ride it | It would flap hard and fly off over the trees at once | It would come closer and sing to you like a blackbird
test: «feathers are for flying, duh, that's literally why» What is better? | Feathers came first for warmth and show, and flight came later | Yes, every feather ever was for flying | Feathers came from fish scales for swimming | Feathers are only for decoration on hats
test: Why did Archaeopteryx in 1861 matter to Darwin's idea? | It was an in-between form, part bird, part dinosaur | It proved that birds had come from fish, not reptiles | It showed that animals never change over time | It was the very first fossil anybody had ever found
test: A scientist finds a tail in amber with feathers on it. Why is amber so good at keeping it? | Resin trapped it and hardened, keeping it in 3D | Amber is a kind of ice that never melts at all | Amber is a kind of bone that grows round things | Amber cooks things hard, like clay in an oven
test: After the asteroid the forests burned. Why did small seed-eating birds have a better chance? | Seeds lasted in the ground and they could live without trees | They could fly up high above the fire and smoke for weeks on end | They ate the burning wood and the ash that fell from it | They were too big and strong for the heat to harm them
test: «penguins have teeth, I saw 'em in a picture» What is true about birds and teeth? | No living bird has real teeth, but some early birds did | All birds have teeth | Only parrots have teeth | Birds never had teeth at any time
test: Watch a chicken strut across the farmyard. Which dinosaur feature can you spot? | Walking on two legs with the body held level | Walking on four legs, like a Triceratops | Crawling along on its belly, like a crocodile | Swinging from branch to branch by its arms
exam: Identify the group of living animals now classified as surviving dinosaurs. | The birds | The lizards | The crocodiles | The amphibians
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Archaeopteryx. | It combined feathered wings with teeth, clawed fingers and a long bony tail | It possessed neither feathers nor wings, and is classified among the lizards | It was a mammal resembling a bat, with wings formed of skin | It was discovered in China in the present century, preserved in amber
exam: Ascertain the anatomical composition of the furcula, commonly termed the wishbone. | Two collarbones fused together | A single rib detached from the chest | A fragment of the skull | A bone of the foot
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the earliest function of feathers? | They are understood to have served first for insulation, and only subsequently for display and flight | They are understood to have served first and solely for flight | They are understood to have served for swimming | They are understood to have served for digging
